/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2255:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:16254:      "docstring": "Stamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.\n- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.\n- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.\n- Stamp each sprite at the placeholder's position inside the frame.\n- Recolor every stamped sprite to the output color defined by the legend.\n\nKey insight: The legend pairs (overlapping two-color glyphs outside the frame) dictate the final recoloring — placeholder color selects the shape, legend maps it to the output color.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2256:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:16375:        "placeholder",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2257:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:16376:        "placeholders",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2258:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:37211:      "docstring": "Recolor each placeholder object according to its normalized shape while leaving the background fixed.\n\nInput structure\nA same-size grid with one dominant background color and one foreground\nplaceholder color forming separated 4-connected objects.\n\nRule\nReplace every placeholder object by the color assigned to its exact\ntranslation-normalized mask; all background pixels remain unchanged.\n\nKey insight\nThe input objects are a shape legend with repeated instances: identical\nmasks always receive identical colors, independent of their position.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2259:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:37215:        "src": "4-connected placeholder-color foreground objects on a dominant background",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/004/codegen_logs/5.log:2260:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:37282:        "placeholder",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2934:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:733:      "docstring": "Assemble colored sprites into a central bar's notches, chaining successive pieces along fixed-width strips.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest monochrome component is a solid \"bar\" with boundary notches (bg holes opening to its bbox edge).\n- Several smaller colored sprites scattered around the bar.\n\nRule:\n- Each bar notch is filled by a root piece whose shape exactly covers the hole.\n- The root defines a strip (column-span or row-span) for its chain.\n- Successive pieces fill the bg cells inside the previous piece's bbox, locked to the same strip width.\n- Multiple chains grow independently from different bar notches.\n\nKey insight: The root piece's extent perpendicular to the chain direction locks the strip width for all subsequent pieces in that chain.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2935:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:825:        "opening",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2936:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:3984:      "docstring": "Eject content pixels from cup-shaped frames outward along each cup's tail direction.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Hollow cup/Y/funnel frames, each a unique border color.\n- Each cup has a convergent tail spike on one side and an open mouth on the opposite side.\n- Content pixels (a single non-border color per cup) sit inside the cup's mouth interior.\n\nRule:\n- For each cup, identify its tail spike and tail direction.\n- Erase all content pixels from the mouth interior.\n- Draw a straight line of content-colored pixels from the spike outward in the tail direction,\n  one pixel per erased content pixel; cells beyond the grid edge are dropped.\n\nKey insight: The tail spike is the unique convergent point whose outward direction is opposite\nthe mouth opening where the content lives.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2937:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:4076:        "opening",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2938:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:4864:        "summary": "Grey horizontal bars with upward projections move upward into semi-open grey slots, aligning projections with slot openings, then colored pixels within slots move upward to align vertically with the bar's projections",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2939:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:4873:        "multi_step": "Step 1: Identify grey bar s with upward projections and grey slot d with colored downward projections; Step 2: Move s upward into d such that s.projections fit into d.openings with projection-to-projection vertical alignment; Step 3: Move colored pixels upward so they align with bar projections, with bar bottom 1 cell above slot bottom",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2940:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:4990:        "openings",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2941:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:9469:      "docstring": "Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n- The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n- Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n- Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n- The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n- Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n\nKey insight:\n- The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2942:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:12884:      "docstring": "Fill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n- A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\nRule:\n- Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n- Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\nKey insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2943:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:12887:        "summary": "A pixel inside a half-surrounded area fills and radiates outward along the border opening, while isolated pixels move to grid edge in the radiate direction, leaving background trail",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2944:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:12892:        "path": "fill the surrounded area and radiate along the surrounded border opening",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2945:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:13003:        "opening",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2946:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:22966:      "docstring": "Recolor wall-separated background regions alternately, starting with grey in the lower-left region.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground wall color.\n- The wall pixels form one or more 4-connected one-cell-thick segments.\n- Each wall segment separates exactly two 4-connected background regions.\nRule\n- Keep all wall pixels unchanged.\n- Label the 4-connected background regions and connect two labels when they\n  touch opposite sides of the same wall segment.\n- Color the region containing the lower-left corner grey, then flip between\n  grey and green across every wall adjacency.\nKey insight\n- The red walls define a bipartite map of regions; the lower-left background\n  region fixes which side of the alternating coloring is grey.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2947:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:22970:        "src": "4-connected orange background regions separated by red one-cell-thick wall segments",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2948:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:35196:      "docstring": "Push the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.\n\nInput structure\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one\n  horizontal or vertical axis.\n- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.\n- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular\n  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.\n\nRule\n- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is\n  flush with the grid edge in that direction.\n- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.\n- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and\n  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.\n- Keep the other end object unchanged.\n\nKey insight\n- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels\n  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2949:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:35285:        "opening",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2950:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:45191:      "docstring": "Connect same-colored foreground components with one-cell-wide orthogonal paths around teal wall segments.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Teal cells are fixed wall segments and are never source connectors.\n- Other foreground colors appear as one or more 4-connected components; colors with a single component stay unchanged.\n\nRule\n- For each non-background, non-teal color, read its 4-connected components in row-major order.\n- Connect each consecutive pair by the shortest clear L-shaped path between the two components.\n- Clear paths may pass only through background or the same color, and they preserve the previous travel axis when both bends are possible.\n\nKey insight\n- The output is not a global recolor: each color traces a routed chain through its own components while teal cells and other colors act as blockers.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2951:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:45196:        "path": "from each component to the next same-colored component in row-major order, take a shortest clear one-bend horizontal/vertical path through background or same color; teal wall segments and other colors block the path",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2952:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:45199:        "transform": "keep teal wall segments, single-component colors, and all original foreground pixels unchanged",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2953:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:45481:      "docstring": "Move the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.",
/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/code/opus/3/005/codegen_logs/6.log:2954:/home/lixinyu/snap/a/key_combo/skill/arc-codegen-similar-local/arc_reference_index.json:45488:        "trajectory": "paint red only at corridor openings flanked by black segments perpendicular to the path",
